---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="About the Bureau | Bureau of Digital Antiquities">
  <section class="page-header">
    <p class="section-label">Institutional Information</p>

    <h1>About the Bureau</h1>

    <p class="page-introduction">
      The Bureau of Digital Antiquities is a public institution dedicated to
      the identification, documentation and preservation of historically
      significant Digital Objects.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="record-section">
    <div class="record-section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">Mandate</p>
      <h2>Mission of the Bureau</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="record-prose">
      <p>
        The Bureau preserves historically significant digital heritage for
        public study, institutional research and future generations.
      </p>

      <p>
        Its work includes provenance research, technical verification,
        archival recovery, preservation monitoring and public publication
        through the Forgotten Web Exchange.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="record-section">
    <div class="record-section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">Institutional History</p>
      <h2>Established 1998</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="record-prose">
      <p>
        The Bureau was established in 1998 in response to the growing need for
        coordinated preservation of historically significant material
        published through emerging digital networks.
      </p>

      <p>
        Its original mandate focused on identifying vulnerable websites,
        documenting their provenance and coordinating preservation activity
        before historically significant material was lost.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="record-section">
    <div class="record-section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">Preservation Principles</p>
      <h2>Digital Heritage as Public Record</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="record-prose">
      <p>
        The Bureau treats historically significant websites, software,
        network services and related digital materials as Digital Objects
        worthy of formal documentation and long-term study.
      </p>

      <p>
        Where continued public accessibility is possible, preservation may be
        conducted in situ. Where material is unavailable or at risk, the
        Bureau may undertake archival recovery, technical reconstruction or
        documentary preservation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="record-section">
    <div class="record-section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">Administration</p>
      <h2>Organisation of the Bureau</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="record-prose">
      <p>
        The Bureau conducts its work through specialised offices, divisions,
        units and archives responsible for preservation, provenance,
        investigation, recovery and publication.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/organisation">
          View the Bureau organisation →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="record-section">
    <div class="record-section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">Public Services</p>
      <h2>Public Access and Research</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="record-prose">
      <p>
        Approved Digital Object Records are made available through the
        Forgotten Web Exchange. The Bureau also publishes Field Reports,
        Preservation Bulletins, Technical Notes and Annual Reports.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/fwx">Browse the FWX Catalogue →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>